From 79e25812c7fb423e69cdcad502a38cd60f25739e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Usty <usty@uase.tech>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 05:48:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] fix: iOS build - commit an actual Package.resolved
 instead of trying to generate one during CI

Two previous attempts at generating Package.resolved fresh during
ci_post_clone.sh both failed to reliably produce a file Xcode's
archive step would accept (once causing the script itself to fail
outright, then even after making that non-fatal, the underlying
resolution still wasn't succeeding - confirmed by the identical
'Configuration Issues... Package.resolved' error persisting in the
next build).

Took a fundamentally different approach: committed an actual, correct
Package.resolved directly to the repo at the exact path Xcode Cloud's
own error message specified. This project has only ONE real remote
Swift Package dependency - capacitor-swift-pm, pinned to exact version
8.4.0 in Package.swift - everything else (CapacitorApp, CapacitorCamera,
etc.) is a local path dependency under node_modules and doesn't need
SPM registry resolution at all. Looked up the exact commit hash for
the 8.4.0 tag directly from GitHub (44ae2505f54a80c5e559533950886d0644fc2fca,
confirmed via git ls-remote, a lightweight tag with no annotation to
dereference) and hand-built a valid version-2 Package.resolved pinning
it precisely.

This sidesteps the CI-generation unreliability entirely - the file is
just there from the moment Xcode Cloud clones the repo, matching
exactly what the error message says is required, rather than depending
on a runtime step to create it correctly. Kept the ci_post_clone.sh
resolution attempt as a non-fatal defensive backup for if a future
plugin gets added without this file being updated, but it's no longer
the primary fix.
